Q
q
1 0 0 1 57.02362 383.7008 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 35.8 Tm /F2 12.2 Tf 16 TL (Q29. Reply to ticket ICICI-4417 in language a non-technical banker will understand.) Tj T* -0.063866 Tw (Explain what moving from spreadsheets to a relational database will fix, what it will) Tj T* 0 Tw (not fix, and what the bank must do differently as a result.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 57.02362 212.7008 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 154.5 Tm /F1 10.5 Tf 15 TL (Moving from spreadsheets to a database gives the bank one controlled place for shared customer and) Tj T* (account information. It reduces duplicate copies of the same information across departments. When an) Tj T* (approved change is made, users can work from the same updated record. The database can prevent) Tj T* (invalid links, such as an account referring to a customer who does not exist. It can also prevent values) Tj T* (such as a negative account balance when that rule is defined. A multi-step banking transaction can be) Tj T* (handled as one complete operation instead of leaving only part of the change. However, the database) Tj T* -0.121885 Tw (will not automatically correct wrong information entered by staff. It will not by itself solve poor processes,) Tj T* 0 Tw (weak access control or poor data governance. The bank therefore needs clear data ownership,) Tj T* -0.10882 Tw (controlled access and proper procedures for entering and checking information. Large reports should be) Tj T* 0 Tw (separated from the day-to-day transaction workload. Backups, monitoring and recovery testing are also) Tj T* (required.) Tj T* ET
